From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ieee1905 0006: let the bridge deliver a unicast we cannot address

i1905_do_cmdu_tx() resolves a unicast destination through the neighbour cache and
then through txpaths, and drops the CMDU when neither knows it. The neighbour
cache only ever learns direct neighbours, so on a chained mesh every node beyond
the first is unreachable this way - even though the L2 path to it exists and
carries data at full rate.

IEEE 1905.1 does not define any forwarding for unicast. Table 6-4 says a received
unicast CMDU is processed and "not transmitted on any of its interfaces", and the
forwarding entity is explicitly out of scope, required only to "be interoperable
with IEEE 802.1 bridging" (P1905.1 WG presentation to IEEE 802.1, Jul 2012,
slides 10 and 23). A unicast CMDU addressed to a device several hops away is
therefore expected to be delivered by the bridge. Refusing to send it is our own
addition, and it is the one that fails.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. Mesh formed as controller <- agentA <- agentB
after a simultaneous restart, agentB two hops out:

  ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"<agentB al-mac>"}'
    -> {"status":"fail"}

  same call with "ifname":"br-lan"
    -> {"status":"ok","mid":19391}
    -> tcpdump on agentB: 3 frames with DA = its own AL MAC arrived,
       3x type 0x0002 Topology Query in and 3x 0x0003 Topology Response out
    -> controller went from num_nodes 2 to 3

The bridge delivers it. The destination AL MAC is in no forwarding database -
it belongs to no netdev of ours, and never appears as a source address, so it
cannot be learned - and an unknown unicast destination is flooded, which reaches
the far node through the intermediary. The same call pinned to the wireless
interface facing the intermediary was accepted and went nowhere: it arrived at
the intermediary and stopped there, which is what a unicast to an unlearned
address does when it is not flooded.

This cannot loop. A CMDU that arrives is processed and never retransmitted, per
the same table, and the bridge applies its own split horizon.

Other open Multi-AP stacks do this unconditionally - no neighbour lookup on
the transmit path at all: the send path resolves the AL MAC to a local netdev
and hands the frame to the kernel, treating the Linux bridge as the
authoritative source of forwarding behaviour. The Broadband Forum reference
implementation makes the same assumption.

What this does not fix: the far node is now reachable and answers, but the
controller still records depth -1 for it. Delivery was only the first of two
problems; placing the node in the topology tree from a Topology Response it did
receive is separate and untouched here.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/i1905_cmdresp.c	2026-07-31 15:58:41.548137614 +0200
+++ b/src/i1905_cmdresp.c	2026-07-31 15:58:41.548215594 +0200
@@ -1074,6 +1074,62 @@
 				i1905_warn(LOG_TX, "%s: cmdu tx failed\n", __func__);
 			}
 		}
+
+		/* Last resort: hand it to the bridge.
+		 *
+		 * Neither the neighbour cache nor the txpaths know this AL MAC, and
+		 * up to now that meant the CMDU was dropped. But 1905.1 defines no
+		 * forwarding for unicast at all - Table 6-4 says a received unicast
+		 * CMDU is processed and "not transmitted on any of its interfaces" -
+		 * and the forwarding entity is explicitly out of scope, required only
+		 * to "be interoperable with IEEE 802.1 bridging". A unicast CMDU to a
+		 * device several hops away is therefore expected to be delivered by
+		 * the bridge, not routed by us. Refusing to send it is our own
+		 * addition, and it is the one that fails.
+		 *
+		 * The neighbour cache only ever learns direct neighbours, so on a
+		 * chained mesh every node beyond the first is unreachable by this
+		 * path even though the L2 route exists and carries data at full rate.
+		 *
+		 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-31, mesh formed as
+		 * controller <- agentA <- agentB:
+		 *
+		 *   ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"<agentB al-mac>"}'
+		 *     -> {"status":"fail"}                      (this branch)
+		 *   same call with "ifname":"br-lan"
+		 *     -> {"status":"ok","mid":19391}
+		 *     -> tcpdump on agentB: 3 frames with DA = its AL MAC arrived,
+		 *        3x type 0x0002 Topology Query in, 3x 0x0003 Response out,
+		 *        controller went from num_nodes 2 to 3.
+		 *
+		 * So the bridge delivers it. The destination AL MAC is not in the
+		 * forwarding database - it belongs to no netdev of ours - and an
+		 * unknown unicast destination is flooded, which reaches the far node
+		 * through the intermediary. That is also why this cannot loop: the
+		 * bridge applies its own split horizon, and a CMDU that does arrive
+		 * is processed and never retransmitted.
+		 *
+		 * Other open 1905 stacks do this unconditionally - no
+		 * neighbour lookup on the transmit path at all - and the
+		 * Broadband Forum reference implementation assumes it.
+		 */
+		if (ret) {
+			list_for_each_entry(iface, &priv->dm.self.iflist, list) {
+				if (iface->exclude || !if_isbridge(iface->ifname))
+					continue;
+
+				ret = i1905_cmdu_tx(iface->priv, vid, dst, src,
+						    type, &mid, data, data_len,
+						    true);
+				if (!ret) {
+					i1905_warn(LOG_TX,
+						   "%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT " handed to bridge %s\n",
+						  __func__, type, MAC2STR(dst),
+						  iface->ifname);
+					goto done;
+				}
+			}
+		}
 	}
 
 done:
